<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  High-frequency sportsbook capture near tip-off, every 2 minutes.

  Exists to answer ONE question from MODELING_FINDINGS.md: when Polymarket
  moves, is the book's old price still available? Our routine captures are
  60.1 minutes apart and the follow-through lands inside that gap, so the
  economics of the cross-venue lead cannot currently be tested at all.

  Installed by scripts/install-market-sync.sh.

  MUST be installed from the MAIN checkout, never a worktree. PLACEHOLDER_REPO
  is filled in from wherever the install script is run, and it was once run
  from a worktree that was later deleted - after which this agent exited 78
  on every fire and the sync agent exited 127, both silently, for weeks.

  2 minutes is chosen from the phenomenon, not from the budget: the
  follow-through is 15-20 minutes wide, so the sampling interval has to be
  a small fraction of it. A 6-hour pre-tip window is ~180 requests per game
  day and a full season is ~18,000 - against a plan holding 5,000,000
  (424 used as of 2026-08-05). Frugality was the wrong axis to optimise:
  the previous design spent 0 requests and produced 0 usable data.

  It still spends nothing when there is no game within 6 hours of tip, so
  the off-season and empty afternoons remain free.

  The capture is followed by log-divergences, which spends NO requests.
  It only reads what was just captured and compares it to the Polymarket
  and Kalshi prices already in the database. That pairing is the point of
  the two-minute cadence: MODELING_FINDINGS.md establishes the divergence
  effect and cannot establish whether the price is still there when you
  could act, because the historical captures were an hour apart.

  Read-only price capture. There is no order placement anywhere in this
  codebase and none should be added (ROADMAP.md non-goals).

  Runs HERE rather than on the capture host, unlike the Kalshi/Polymarket
  recorder. That one records feeds whose quotes are unrecoverable; this one
  reads a paid API with a shared quota, and pointing two machines at the
  same key would double the burn while halving nobody's uncertainty.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.ostepan.wnba-odds-focused</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/bin/zsh</string>
        <string>-lc</string>
        <string>cd PLACEHOLDER_REPO &amp;&amp; uv run wnba-engine capture-odds-focused &amp;&amp; uv run wnba-engine log-divergences</string>
    </array>

    <!-- 2 minutes. -->
    <key>StartInterval</key>
    <integer>120</integer>

    <!-- Deliberately NOT RunAtLoad. Unlike the capture agent, a missed
         window here is not lost data - the routine hourly snapshot still
         runs - so there is no reason to fire an extra request on every
         reload while iterating on the plist. -->
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <false/>

    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>PLACEHOLDER_HOME/wnba-market-capture/logs/odds-focused.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>PLACEHOLDER_HOME/wnba-market-capture/logs/odds-focused.log</string>

    <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
    <string>PLACEHOLDER_REPO</string>
</dict>
</plist>
